TI  - The impact of green spruce aphid, Elatobium abietinum (Walker), on the growth of young Sitka spruce in Hafren Forest, Wales: pattern of defoliation and effect on shoot growth
T2  - FOREST ECOLOGY AND MANAGEMENT
AB  - Green spruce aphid (Elatobium abietinum) is the main defoliator of Sitka spruce in the UK, but there is a lack of quantitative data on its effects on tree growth which can be used to predict its impact on current and future timber yields. Consequently, in 1992, the UK Forestry Commission set up a long-term field experiment in Hafren Forest, Wales, to determine the effects of E. abietinum, and root aphids (Pachypappa and Pachypappella spp.), on the growth of young Sitka spruce plantations. The experiment included four treatments in which natural populations of E. abietinum and root aphids were maintained or were excluded by insecticides, and a fifth treatment in which E. abietinum densities were raised artificially. Natural aphid populations were low during 1992-1995, precluding analysis of the impact of root aphids, but E. abietinum in the enhanced population treatment reached, in June 1994, densities equivalent to a moderate-severe natural outbreak (35-90 aphids.100 needles(-1) on upper shoots). Needle fall was strongly correlated with E. abietinum density and the heavily infested trees lost, on average, 38.5% of their older (1991-1993) needles. This was associated with a 22-23% reduction in leader length in 1994 and a 11-13% reduction in tree height. Aphids and defoliation were concentrated at the tops of the trees and decreased toward the bases, and a similar pattern was observed in relative reductions in shoot growth. This suggested a local influence of aphids or defoliation on shoot growth within the canopy, but such effects could not be distinguished from a response of the trees to the overall rate of damage. Changes in needle size following infestation were not correlated with shoot growth and indicated a differential effect of aphid infestation on shoot and needle development. Continued monitoring of the experiment over the next 2-3 yr should reveal whether further, longer-term changes in shoot and needle production contribute to the restoration of normal tree growth. Crown copyright (C) 1998 Published by Elsevier Science B.V. All rights reserved.
