AU  - OLCKERS, T
AU  - HULLEY, PE
TI  - IMPOVERISHED INSECT HERBIVORE FAUNAS ON THE EXOTIC BUGWEED SOLANUM-MAURITIANUM SCOP RELATIVE TO INDIGENOUS SOLANUM SPECIES IN NATAL KWAZULU AND THE TRANSKEI
T2  - JOURNAL OF THE ENTOMOLOGICAL SOCIETY OF SOUTHERN AFRICA
AB  - The exotic invader Solanum mauritianum Scop. (bugweed) is attacked by mainly polyphagous insects in Natal/KwaZulu and the Transkei. There were more herbivores and higher levels of damage on indigenous Solanum species than on S. mauritianum. The herbivore community of Solanum panduriforme E.Mey., the most abundant of these, comprised mainly oligophagous species, many of which occurred on the other solanums examined. Very few oligophagous insects transferred from their indigenous Solanum hosts to S. mauritianum. The occurrence of these herbivores was localized and sporadic, with little resultant damage to the plant. The situation was very similar to that reported earlier for S. mauritianum in the eastern Cape, indicating that the herbivore faunas of indigenous Solanum species are not preadapted to utilize S. mauritianum in South Africa. A major factor may be the absence of any close relatives (at the subgeneric level) of the well-defended S. mauritianum in Africa. S. mauritianum is thus a promising candidate for biological control by introduced agents.
