AU  - Dhileepan, K
AU  - Setter, SD
AU  - McFadyen, RE
TI  - Impact of defoliation by the biocontrol agent Zygogramma bicolorata on the weed Parthenium hysterophorus in Australia
T2  - BIOCONTROL
AB  - The leaf-feeding beetle Zygogramma bicolorata Pallister was introduced from Mexico into Australia in 1980 as a biocontrol agent for the weed Parthenium hysterophorus L. (Asteraceae). Z. bicolorata became abundant in 1990, and since 1992 there has been regular outbreaks resulting in the defoliation of the weed in central Queensland. In this study we evaluated the impact of defoliation by Z. bicolorata on P. hysterophorus from 1996 to 1998. Z. bicolorata caused 91-100% defoliation resulting in reductions in weed density by 32-93%, plant height by 18-65%, plant biomass by 55-89%, flower production by 75-100%, soil seed-bank by 13-86% and seedling emergence in the following season by 73-90%. At sites with continued outbreaks of Z. bicolorata, it is expected that the existing soil seed-bank will be minimised, resulting in reduced density of parthenium in 6 to 7 years.
