TI  - AN EARLY ASSESSMENT OF TRICHAPION-LATIVENTRE (COLEOPTERA, APIONIDAE) FOR BIOLOGICAL-CONTROL OF THE WEED SESBANIA-PUNICEA (FABACEAE) IN SOUTH-AFRICA
T2  - JOURNAL OF THE ENTOMOLOGICAL SOCIETY OF SOUTHERN AFRICA
AB  - Surveys on the introduced apionid weevil Trichapion lativentre showed that adult and larval feeding damage inhibited the growth of the South American weed Sesbania punicea and reduced seed-set of the plants by more than 80% on average in the East London area, South Africa. Intraspecific competition for food and breeding sites occurred on one occasion when there were over six adult weevils per 300 mm of branch in spring, with the following results, (i) the weevils caused extensive damage to the new plant growth and no pods were set by the plants throughout the summer, (ii) there was a population crash instead of an increase in weevil numbers early in the summer, (iii)there was a decrease in the average size of the few adult weevils that were produced, (iv) low numbers of small adults survived to the following spring and (v) the plants suffered less damage from T. lativentre at the start of the next summer. The weevils were therefore unable to sustain complete inhibition of  seed-set for two consecutive years. Trichapion lativentre acting alone will probably not be able to completely check the spread of S. punicea and two additional herbivorous weevil species have been introduced for biological control of the weed.
