ED  - Pergl, J
TI  - A tale of two continents: the role of ecology and evolution in a biological invasion
T2  - BIOLOGICAL INVASIONS: TOWARDS A SYNTHESIS, PROCEEDINGS
CP  - 5th European Conference on Biological Invasion - Neobiota
AB  - The perennial plant Silene latifolia has emerged as a model system for investigating the evolutionary and ecological basis of biological invasions. This paper synthesizes the studies that have used a variety of approaches (field observational, experimental, molecular) that together provide a picture of how species may shift from being benign in their native range to problematic upon introduction to a novel habitat. This European species was introduced to North America about 200 years ago. Common garden experiments conducted on both continents reveal that there has been a significant evolutionary shift in life history traits, as the North American plants consistently express a more aggressive phenotype. A large-scale survey of populations across the species' range in Europe and North America found enemy attack by generalists (floral herbivory) and specialists (fruit predation, fungal disease) to be much higher in the native range. We believe that this release from enemy attack has played a major role in Silene's evolution. North American plants invest less into potential defensive mechanisms (trichomes and fruit wall) and are more susceptible to being attacked by the specialist enemies upon reintroduction to a common garden in Europe. Taken together, these patterns suggest that natural selection has favoured a reallocation of energy from defensive traits to those conferring an advantage in overall growth and performance (e.g., flower and fruit production). However, there is also evidence that the colonization history has influenced the biology of the species in North America. Significant genetic structure in Europe along with an apparent non-random introduction of the more aggressive Central European phenotype may indicate that a founder event of a pre-adapted genotype occurred. In sum, our work demonstrates how ecological and evolutionary forces act in concert to influence the outcome of species' translocations.
