TI  - EFFECTS OF BURNING ON A DESERT GRASS-SHRUB RANGE IN SOUTHERN ARIZONA
T2  - ECOLOGY
AB  - Invasion and increase of mesquite (Prosopis spp.), cactus (Opuntia spp.), and burro-weed (Aplopappus tenuisectus) on many rangelands in southern Arizona is causing a serious loss in grazing capacity and livestock production. This paper reports the effect of a controlled broadcast burn on the above undesirable species and desirable perennial grasses. The fire reduced burro-weed by about nine-tenths, cholla by about half, prickly pear by about one-fourth, and mesquite by about one-tenth. Reduction in density and herbage production of all desirable perennial grasses resulting from burning required from two to four growing seasons for restoration. Black grama, the perennial grass most seriously damaged by burning had not completely recovered after five growing seasons. Burning released some of the soluble salts and nitrogen in the soil.
