TI  - POPULATION-DYNAMICS OF JAPANESE HEMLOCK SCALES - A COMPARISON OF ENDEMIC AND EXOTIC COMMUNITIES
T2  - ECOLOGY
AB  - The dynamics of endemic populations of two armored scales, Fiorinia externa and Nuculaspis (= Tsugaspidiotus) tsugae (Homoptera: Diaspididae), were studied in Japan and compared with the dynamics of introduced populations of these scales in the United States. In Japan both scales were distributed widely among the 13 natural and cultivated stands of Tsuga diversifolia and T. sieboldii sampled throughout Honshu, but scale densities were innocuous and low relative to those in North  America. Density, survivorship, and fecundity of both scale species were significantly higher on hemlocks planted outside their natural range in Japan than on naturally occurring montane trees, and signficantly higher on Tsuga species exotic to Japan than on Japanese hemlocks. I infer from this that trees growing outside their natural habitats are more susceptible to insect herbivores, presumably due to stress from less adequate growing conditions. Experiments conducted in a field plot in  Kyoto, Japan revealed that on each hemlock species scale fecundity was always higher among scales that had been transferred from another individual of that same hemlock species than among scales that had been transferred from an individual of a different hemlock species. These results supported those from a similar experiment involving exotic scale populations in Connecticut, USA, and indicated that scales residing on adjacent trees may be qualitatively different. Data from the field plot study  in Kyoto and life table data gathered from 13 sites in Honshu revealed that endemic populations have the potential to attain densities injurious to host hemlocks, especially trees in cultivation. However, this seldom occurs because to hymenopterous parasitoids, Aspidiotiphagus citrinus and Arrhenophagus albitibiae, regularly killed from 90.2 to 94.2% of both scale species. Throughout Honshu the seasonal occurrence of the bivoltine adult parasitoids and vulnerable stages of bivoltine F. externa and N. tsugae were synchronous, resulting in high parasitism rates and a low range of flucuation in endemic scale populations. In contrast, Connecticut populations of As. citrinus are asynchronous with their scale host, F. externa, which is univoltine there. The resulting higher populations of the scale in Connecticut injure and kill their new host plant, Tsuga canadensis.
