TI  - Nonlinear grazing effects on vegetation biomass and soil properties in a desert grassland
T2  - ECOLOGICAL FRONTIERS
AB  - Desert grasslands are essential components of dryland ecosystems, providing critical ecosystem services and supporting livestock production. However, they are highly susceptible to degradation due to climatic and human influences. While current research has examined grazing impacts, the nonlinear effects of grazing intensity on soil- vegetation interactions remain poorly understood. This study addresses this gap by indentifying ecological thresholds beyond which desert grassland ecosystems undergo abrupt deterioration. We investigated the desert grasslands of Urad Middle Banner, Bayannur, Inner Mongolia, China, using vegetation and soil sampling along with a questionnaire survey, to assess grazing intensity effect. Our analysis, incorporating correlation analysis and a threshold regression model, revealed that grazing intensity has nonlinear effects on both vegetation and soil. When the grazing intensity exceeds 270 sheep units per kilometre, abrupt decreases in aboveground biomass, total potassium, and alkalihydrolysed nitrogen occur. Similarly, at a threshold of 68 sheep units per kilometre, sudden increases in soil organic carbon and sudden decreases in the root-to-shoot biomass ratio are observed. These thresholds mark critical transition points, beyond which ecosystems feedback mechanisms accelerate irreversible degradation. Our findings offer a novel perspective on early diagnostic indicators of desert grassland degradation and provide a scientific basis for sustainable grazing management. By identifying grazing limits that maintain ecosystem stability, this study informs ecological restoration efforts and policy frameworks aimed at mitigating desertification risks in drylands.
