TI  - EFFECTS OF COPPER AND GREEN ALGAE SUPPLEMENTATION ON OVARIAN FUNCTION, BLOOD AND METABOLIC PROFILES OF BOER GOATS GRAZING COPPER-DEFICIENT ALFALFA IN ARID SUBTROPICS
T2  - FRESENIUS ENVIRONMENTAL BULLETIN
AB  - Copper deficiency is a common problem leading to adverse effects on productive and reproductive performance of ruminant animals. The aims of present study were to restore ovarian activity, blood indices and milk production of goats grazing copper-deficient alfalfa upon copper and/or Chlorella vulgaris microalgae supplementation. Forty pregnant Boer goats (38.10 +/- .70 kg body weight) were distributed to four groups; Chlorella vulgaris microalgae (CV; 10.0 g/head/day), copper (Cu; 15.0 ml/head/day copper sulfate), a mixture of both CV and Cu (CVCu) and control groups. Ovarian ftmctions, blood profiles and milk yield of Boer goats grazing copper-deficient alfalfa were determined. The numbers and sizes of ovarian follicles and corpora lutea in addition to pregnancy rate were increased (P < 0.05) due to CV and/or Cu supplementation. Values of blood indices (RBCs, WBCs, Hb and PCV), physiological parameters, metabolic parameters (total protein, albumin, glucose, urea and total cholesterol) and hormones (T3, T4, estrogen, progesterone) were significantly improved in CV and/or Cu groups. Supplementation with CVCu increased (P < 0.05) milk yield and its total solid and energy. In conclusion, productive and reproductive performances could be improved as results of copper and Chlorella vulgaris administration to Boer goats grazing copper-deficient alfalfa in arid subtropics.
